-- One constrained call NESTED inside another, executed
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §6.2 Rule U1, §8.2, A.57, A.75).
--
-- `keepLarger (keepLarger empty "b") "q"` instantiates `keepLarger` twice,
-- and the inner call's result `Box k1` meets the outer call's parameter
-- `Box k2` only after BOTH `k.compare` constraints are in the table —
-- which is what the `let`-bound `empty` buys: the receiver is still a
-- variable when the second call is instantiated, so Rule U1 has two
-- constraints to join on one root rather than one already-discharged one.
--
-- A join COPIES its inputs onto a fresh range, because a constraint set is
-- a range of an append-only table and is never edited (§6.1 invariant 2).
-- Before A.75 the copy and the input it superseded were two live
-- obligations over one site list, so each call wrote its site twice and
-- the emitted call would have passed the comparator twice:
-- `Lower.evidenceShapeOk` counts two top-level slots against the one
-- parameter `keepLarger` declares and stops the build with `internal`,
-- which is what this program did on a checker without the redirect.
--
-- The same nesting through `Dict.insert` is `run/Dictionaries`; this
-- fixture owns the shape with a type of its own, so the pin stays put when
-- `core/Dict` moves. `dispatch/JoinedConstraintSites` is the table
-- underneath it, and the reason both exist is that this one cannot see the
-- difference between a right table and a table that is right twice over.
--
-- Two deep and three deep, because the chain is what the join walks; the
-- `reversed` line puts the winner in the OTHER operand, so a comparator
-- wired to the wrong argument still has to disagree with one of them; and
-- `numbers` reaches the same join with the receiver still a `number`,
-- through the A.53 bridge rather than through a structure.
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


pub type Box k
    = Empty
    | Holding k


pub keepLarger : Box k, k → Box k
    where k.compare : k, k → Order
keepLarger box candidate =
    case box of
        Empty →
            Holding candidate

        Holding held →
            if held < candidate then
                Holding candidate
            else
                Holding held


unwrap : Box k, k → k
unwrap box fallback =
    case box of
        Empty →
            fallback

        Holding held →
            held


main : Program
main =
    empty = Empty

    letters = keepLarger (keepLarger (keepLarger empty "b") "q") "f"

    reversed = keepLarger (keepLarger empty "z") "a"

    numbers = keepLarger (keepLarger empty 5) 3
    Node.printLines
        [ unwrap letters "?"
        , unwrap reversed "?"
        , String.fromInt (unwrap numbers 0)
        ]
